Let PA=x and PD=y. Since the two secants have equal chord lengths, PB=x+8 and PC=y+8. Power of P gives x(x+8)=y(y+8), and positivity implies x=y. Also O is equidistant from the two equal chords, so PO bisects angle APC. In triangle APC, the angle-bisector theorem gives AL/LC=PA/PC=x/(x+8)=1/5; hence x=2, so AP=2.

For part (b), the power relation with radius 5 gives OP²−25=PA·PB=2·10=20, so OP=3√5. The perpendicular projection of O onto either chord has distance 3 from its midpoint, so in the right triangle formed with half-chord 4, the distance from O to each chord is 3. Equivalently, if φ is half of angle APC, then OP cosφ=PA+4=6; thus cosφ=2/√5 and sinφ=1/√5. Therefore sin∠APC=sin(2φ)=4/5. The area of APC is (1/2)(2)(10)(4/5)=8. Its inradius is r=8/((2+10+4√5)/2)=3−√5. Since T lies on the angle bisector at P, PT=r/sinφ=√5(3−√5)=3√5−5.

FINAL_ANSWER: \boxed{\text{(a) }2;\quad \text{(b) }PT=3\sqrt5-5,\;[ACP]=8}

